  "notes": "London's most harmonically sophisticated guitar writer, and the entry says the harmony *is* the parameter: \"guitar-voiced extended chords with altered tensions (maj7#11, m11, 13ths, add9 with open strings), alternate tunings, modal borrowing, and progressions of 6 or 8 bars that resolve unexpectedly. Both major and minor tonics, often ambiguous.\" So no progression families are authored — an unexpected resolution is precisely what a fixed family cannot express — and the delta is a near-zero triad weight, the second-highest ninth weight in this batch, a large `susSubstitutionProb` for the altered tensions and short chord durations for the frequent movement. Drums are \"a light live kit — brushes or soft sticks, snare on 2&4 with a short decay, ride rather than hats, ghost strokes throughout; a substantial part of the catalogue is voice-and-guitar with no drums at all, and tempo is elastic within a take\". Elastic tempo is `quantizeStrength` 0.42, the lowest of the modern artists here; ride-not-hats is a hat fill density of 0.1 against a ride lane at 2–5 hits a bar; \"ghost strokes throughout\" is a 0.48 ghost probability on the four e-positions. Melody is \"soprano-light with a wide range, moderate note-density, long melismatic tails, and close-harmony self-stacks\" — the melismatic tails are `endVariation` and the raised octave jump. Because \"electric or acoustic guitar played fingerstyle is both the harmony and the countermelody\", the counter is an `arp` at a high density ratio. Bass is \"sparse and low in the mix, entering after the guitar establishes the figure\". ⚠ The INFERRED parameters are every drum number and all melody/countermelody figures; the research marks tempo and drum values as inference and [LOW-CONF].",
